            <ArticleTitle>Antihyperalgesic activity of verbascoside in two models of neuropathic pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>594-601</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.2042-7158.2011.01264.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This study reports on the rapid isolation of verbascoside from Lippia citriodora H.B.K. (Verbenaceae), an inexpensive and widespread source, and the evaluation of its antihyperalgesic activity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Isolation of verbascoside was achieved by size exclusion chromatography with Sephadex LH-20 eluting with 50% EtOH, which is proposed as a fast and efficient method of separation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="KEY FINDINGS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The antihyperalgesic activity of verbascoside was tested by in-vivo assay using the paw-pressure test in two animal models of neuropathic pain: a peripheral mononeuropathy produced either by a chronic constriction injury of the sciatic nerve (CCI) or by an intra-articular injection of sodium monoiodoacetate (MIA).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Verbascoside administered intraperitoneally at a dose of 100 mg/kg reverted the mechanical hyperalgesia in both CCI and MIA treated rats, as evaluated in the paw-pressure test. Verbascoside was also effective against mechanical hyperalgesia after oral administration at doses of 300 and 600 mg/kg.</AbstractText>
